d to the other beads  and if you want to you can pick  igs that are specific for either ig  subtypes  or the igg that you're interested in and  then this will bind to the antibodies  you can come in with sap for this one  and then in each well of a 96 well plate  this can then be read in one of our  luminex instruments  where each well is going to be a similar  sample measuring multiple antibody  titers depending on how many different  beads  you have in there with the different  antigens with up to 500 maximum  now if you want to look at different ig  subtypes in this type of assay  then each sample would require  additional wells with the detection  reagent specific for that ig class you  want to measure  processing of the assay can take  anywhere from just a few hours to  several hours  depending on the sensitivity and how the  assay was designed  that generally will encapsulate taking  your beads mixing with sample  for a specified period of time doing  several washes adding the detection  reagent  several more washes and then reading one  of our instruments  so this type of approach has already  been used with the luminex platform for  a number of different pathogens  not just the coronavirus that we looked  at earlier it's also been used in a  variety of animal model systems  to look at the presence of titers  against various animal  animal viruses and other pathogens in  bovine livestock  and various companion animals it's also  been used in vaccine testing  that is exemplified by this measles  mumps rubella  paracel study here  it's also been used in uh as immune  surveillance  in this case studying of a population  exposed to different malaria  parasites  so santa body titers have also been  developed by a number of  uh partners that we work with both  academic  as well as corporate and some state  laboratories  one of which is the university of  rochester in rochester new york  there's central laboratory and dr pecora  has data on that  dr pakora i'll turn that over to you  hi thanks steve yes i'm going to jump in  for a few slides and talk about  the covid19 serological assay that we  have developed here  in our clinical diagnostic laboratory on  the luminex platform  so this assay is a five-plex assay  of the five bead regions three of them  are coronavirus specific so  we purchased all of our antigens from a  commercial supplier  in this case it was genscript the three  coronavirus antigens we used were the  full spike protein that was expressed in  insect cells  we also used the rbd by itself the  receptor binding domain  as well as the nucleocapsid protein  expressed  in e coli the rbd and nuclear capsids  were coupled at equimolar concentrations  in this case both 100 picomoles per  million beads  whereas the spike was somewhat lower at  10.  we also included two control beads we  have an igg control  which is simply a different bead region  that was coupled with  total human igg and we use this to  monitor the addition of anti-igg  detection reagents  and finally our fifth bead is an  instrument control bead which is  internally dyed and is there to  basically monitor the  machine performance  so to bring this assay up and to develop  it we had to optimize a few different  aspects once we had the antigen  we had to assess different antigen  coupling concentrations  to confirm the coupling we had to  assess a few different antibodies from  commercial sources  to be sensitive enough to detect that um  once we had our bees coupled and the  coupling confirmed  protocols are worked out we had to then  assess  a good detection antibody so we looked  at several biotin labeled or  pe directly conjugated anti-human iggs  and then of course we also had to  optimize the  serum dilutions we were going to use in  this case we ended up picking  one to 2000 which equates to about 10  microliters of total serum input so it's  you need very little serum for this  assay  and then finally once those things are  optimized for the assay itself we ended  up  deciding on a combination of biotin  labeled anti-human igg  along with sape but we add them together  in one step so that having the two  separate reagents allows us  to pretty to fine tune in order to  minimize background and enhance  sensitivity by adding them both together  we then make it a more efficient  assay to run and in a clinical  laboratory  the speed of the assay and the  efficiency is is obviously  quite important so this slide cartoons  the general workflow of the assay  once the serum is alloquoted the whole  workflow is about 45 minutes  so this includes an incubation step  where we incubate the  bees along with the serum for 15 minutes  at 37 degrees  we wash it two times each wash is about  two minutes  and then we incubate with our detection  reagents together as i said before in  one step  so that's another 15 minutes two more  washes and then we  read it on the flexmap 3d platform  once the plate has gone through all that  and is red then  before we even decide whether or not we  will look at the patient samples we  assess all of the controls so there are  several controls integrated  in each plate these include negative  negative sample controls including both  pbs  tbn so just buffer as well as igg  stripped  human serum there are also two positive  sample controls we incorporate on every  plate this includes a low positive  patient and a high positive patient  and then as i said a couple slides ago  we have our human igg coated bees  and our internal control beads so for us  to accept this plate and  and to look at any of the patient  samples all of these controls have to be  within range  um if any of them are out then the plate  is not is not  accepted we we established the ranges by  running many hundreds of samples through  the validation procedure  um in order to to get all of those  quality control  criteria uh determined  so once all that was established then  our next question was to decide how we  were going to threshold and  uh differentiate negative from positive  calls  in going into this assay our biggest  concern really was specificity  even more so than sensitivity so  although we are in new york we are  actually in a region of somewhat low  cova 19 prevalence  going into this we estimated our  prevalence was probably about three  percent  based on some serious prevalence work  done by our public health laboratory and  also our molecular diagnostics  numbers and so with a prevalence at  three percent  um a specificity really needs to be as  close to  100 percent as possible in order to have  a very  high positive protective value um  as mentioned here so clearly 97 percent  you're at the three percent prevalence  your your ppv is quite low so  again our goal was to get as close to  100 as possible  to assess this we used a set of pre-2020  serum so we had this banked in our  clinical laboratory  and it has been sent to us for a variety  of diagnostic regions i'll go  through that in the next slide and then  after running through these samples we  set our thresholds for  s rbd and n separately that were all  above any sample in that pre-covet set  so that's what i'm showing on this slide  here so our we chose our pre-covered  serum  to address a number of different  conditions which are known to  have potential cross-reactivity and  immunoassays  these include serum from patients with  positive ana titers  dmv ebv lyme rheumatoid factor and  syphilis  as well as serum from patients with a  known acute respiratory infection from  you know other viruses as well as many  serum from patients  whose serum was submitted to our  laboratory and it was negative for  anything that it had been tested for  so by going through about 215 samples  this is the data we got so in the  columns on this slide  i'm showing you the average mfi across  that group  and in parentheses i'm showing you the  maximum mfi because that is how we  threshold  based on the maximum and what you 