 in conclusion for on my section we  found that the sars cov2 multiplexed  assay was highly sensitive and specific  and compared quite favorably to the  abbott architect cma  e-way method which we think is also  extremely good the results are highly  reproducible between runs and users  for our clinical lab that's very  important i mean we found that it really  was sufficiently robust for clinical use  and i should point out that actually  this  assay was our first luminex essay we  brought the technology in specifically  for this assay so  we found that it was compatible with a  clinical  environment and one of the things we  really liked about it and one of the  reasons we went to it is because  we really wanted the ability to  customize the antigens  to allow for flexibility as we all know  knowledge about the immune response to  stars cov2  is evolving rapidly and we wanted to be  able to be fairly nimble to address that  furthermore the large dynamic range of  the assay allows for us to develop  quantitatively to assess you know for  example donors have convalescent plasma  and to monitor recipients of both plasma  and vaccines  um the assay i showed you today was  validated as a qualitative assay but we  are intending to validate it further as  a quantitative  and with that i'm going to turn it back  over to steve and  i'll be happy to accept any questions at  the end  thank you nicole so  as we progress through this pandemic  this type of assay  can still have use as we go into the  post-pandemic phase  for one thing as it spread globally we  started to see that the symptoms for  people with coronavirus resemble flu as  well as pneumonia-like infections  so as a result it's possible that you  know there could be a small population  that if they turn up positive or  negative rather for say  the pcr assay they may have infections  caused by other pathogens  so having a serological assay you know  that can look for these other pathogens  which could be  viruses 